This source package is built from the upstream release asset
0c-ime-<version>.tar.xz (not the "Source code" archive generated by GitHub).
That asset includes assets/data/ with the compiled dictionary and
models/pairing.lock.json; a plain Git checkout does not, so run
"make assets MODELS=0" before testing a build from Git.

Dictionary source
  assets/data is compiled from the Git data plus four bigram TSVs.  They ship
  as the component tarball 0c-ime_<version>.orig-bigram.tar.xz (about 307 MB),
  which debian/watch fetches from the 0c-ime-data GitHub release, so they are
  part of the Debian source package under bigram/.  Each line holds only
  prev-word, word and P(word|prev); no original text can be recovered.
  The build still uses the prebuilt assets/data.  Upstream rebuilt it with
  scripts/build_release_data.py --bigram-dir and got byte-identical output
  for 0.2.2; this has not been re-run for 0.2.6.  The component's version is
  ignored, so uscan takes the newest bigram release: when updating, check
  its checksums against data/sources/bigram.lock.json.

Go modules
  The build stays in module mode (GO111MODULE=on, GOFLAGS=-mod=readonly,
  GOPROXY=off) and debian/patches/0002 replaces the four modules with the
  Debian-packaged sources under /usr/share/gocode/src.  Module mode matters:
  go.mod's go directive selects net/http's Go 1.22+ ServeMux behavior; GOPATH
  mode falls back to the old mux and the local API routes return 404.
  Versions in sid: go-winio 0.6.2, golang-dbus 5.1.0, uax29/v2 2.7.0,
  x/sys 0.49.0.  Recheck them when updating the package.  The Go binaries
  are built with CGO_ENABLED=1 and -buildmode=pie for Debian; the official
  packages use CGO_ENABLED=0 for portability (debian/patches/0004).

Settings application
  The 0c-settings Tauri application is not built in this package.  Its
  Cargo.lock has 455 crates and Debian sid has no librust-tauri/wry/tao
  packages, so shipping it in main would need a vendored Rust tree, which the
  Rust team does not accept for main.  The engine and both desktop shells work
  without it, but the Fcitx5 menu entry and the IBus <setup> command call
  "0c-update open-settings" and report that it is missing.  The
  model-download reminder would do the same, so debian/patches/0006 skips it
  while no settings program is installed; "0c-update models download" still
  works.  debian/patches/0005 lets "0c-update profile" run on a
  package-managed install; without it new users cannot change the keys locked
  by the built-in input style.  Both are to be fixed upstream.

Neural runtime
  python3, python3-numpy and python3-onnxruntime are only Suggested: they
  serve the optional neural models, which are not in main.  Without them the
  engine reports runtime_missing and keeps using statistical selection.
  Upstream pins numpy 2.5.3 and onnxruntime 1.30.0; sid's numpy 2.4.6 and
  onnxruntime 1.23 import and expose the APIs the workers use, but inference
  with the real models has not been tested yet.

Tests
  "go test -count=1 ./..." runs during the package build with the same
  module replacements, unless DEB_BUILD_OPTIONS contains nocheck.  One
  settingsapp test that checks system-file ownership is skipped for root
  builds (debian/patches/0003); Debian buildds run unprivileged.  The
  autopkgtest additionally checks that the packaged dictionary loads with
  "0c-server -check-data".

Neural models
  The models are non-free (MIT weights, but no training source) and are
  packaged separately as 0c-ime-models in the non-free archive.  They install
  to /usr/share/0c/neural/{select,punct} and are detected as bundled models.
  debian/control will gain "Suggests: 0c-ime-models" once that package is in
  the archive.

ITP: https://bugs.debian.org/1152241 (closed by the first upload).
Packaging lives on the debian/latest branch of the upstream repository
(DEP-14 layout); tag uploads as debian/<version>.
